Jason hare he is the director of ESPN's  the last dance  a six-time Emmy award-winning director  and producer of his 10 part documentary  series the last dance well this kind of  snuck up on me I didn't know that there  was going to be a documentary on the  Chicago Bulls Jason how did you keep it  a secret you know we've been speaking in  code for the last years and and just  this week people seem to have gotten the  message okay when did this concept take  place in the offseason after the Bulls  won their fifth title in seven years in  the summer of 1997 a guy named Andy  Thompson who has been a producer with  NBA entertainment for decades and he's a  great guy he's Klay Thompson's Uncle  Michael Thompson's brother he knew  Michael and the Bulls and he saw the  writing on the wall Jerry Krause said to  Phil Jackson you can go 82 and oh and  you're not coming back to coach this  team next year and Michael Jordan had  been very publicly adamant that he  wasn't going to play for anybody but  Phil Jackson so the writing was on the  wall that this was going to be the last  season that this core was going to be  together so Andy Thompson went to his  van boss Adam Silver who was the  president of MB entertainment at the  time and said we should follow this team  around we should embed a crew just  because this is going to be historic and  we need this footage for posterity they  both thought that was a great idea and  they went to Michael unfilled but they  said yes and the footage has not seen  the light of day for 22 years but you'll  see it on Sunday night that's great  am I in the documentary your voice is  yes your voice and your face are um at  various times telling the story of  Michael you know there's ample footage  that we were allowed from ESPN and from  the NBA everybody kind of opened their  vault here so you have a presence Nariko  solutely residuals I'm guessing who do I  talk to about that  you certainly don't talk to the people  who are paying me because I'm well then  I'm not getting any if he has fans not  paying you I your first meeting with  Jordan described it I got a call at 6:30  on a Wednesday night from SD Portnoy who  is his longtime manager and I have been  working with SD and Curtis Pope who is  his business partner for the better part  of a year preparing for you know just  the creative elements and the  storytelling structure that this series  was going to entail and I hadn't met  Michael yet I've never been in the same  room as Michael Jordan at that point  despite 20 years in the business I just  never crossed paths with him  he was from a different generation than  the one that I worked in so she said  Michael would like to have a drink and  they were at a hotel in midtown  Manhattan I was a lot more than a half  hour away but managed to move heaven and  earth to get up there in time to sit  down with him and meet him for the first  time how cooperative was he for wanting  to do this he was well one of the first  questions I asked him is why now why do  you want to do this and he said I don't  and I said why not and he said because  when you see some of this footage it's  so raw that I don't know that people  will be able to understand without  context why I was acting the way I acted  why I had to be that intense in practice  why I had to make every practice more  difficult in any game that the Bulls  could possibly experience against the  indiana's and the Miami's and the New  York's of the eastern conference back in  those days I think when people see just  a clip of me getting on a teammate or  riding Scottie Burrell or yelling at Ron  Harper or Steve Kerr they're not going  to understand that and I'm a little bit  hesitant about that so I said you the  benefit of 10 hours to tell this story  and we have the benefit of time and  resources and context and we'll be able  to sit down and you can explain to me  exactly what you just explained to me  and give that context to explain to  people why you had to do this and we'll  also interview the teammates that were  on the other end of that behavior and  get their perspective on it so I think  once he was comfortable that we were  going to tell an honest responsible  comprehend  Noorie and that's you know good bad and  the ugly warts and all he was all in and  he was adamant from that first day and  and you know to two nights ago when he's  still watching rough cuts we're not done  with this series yet but everybody is in  quarantine so even Michael Jordan is  watching rough cuts of the Bulls Michael  Jordan documentary and he was adamant up  until two nights ago that we include  certain things that I thought well he  may not want this in there he might not  want this in there he wants this to be  as honest and truthful a telling you  this story as possible so it's been a  gift that he's been this cooperative so  you have eight parts done and two that  are still being done we're first and  goal from the one inch line on Episode  six and seven we're in the red zone on  Episode eight nine and and we're moving  the ball down the field we're over  midfield on ten but we'll get there in  time for may 17 I give you a credit  though Jase because I can't imagine if  I'm Jordan why I would want to do this  so I'm not surprised that that was  initial reaction but then you tap into  the competitor who now wants it to be  the greatest documentary of all time I'm  going to guess yeah I think that with  him if it's if it's if it's the team  that he plays on in the NBA being the  best if it's the documentary he  participates in being the best if it's  the breakfast cereal that he's choosing  to eat before he goes out golfing he  wants that to be the best everything  from the time that before this guy  picked up a basketball that was  instilled in him by his parents James  and Delores you do your best at all  times what did he want to talk what did  he love talking about  he liked he lit up he talked about his  family when he talked about his mom in  the first episode we used this technique  where I he doesn't know me so it's  better for him to see these people  saying these things about him and that  goes from Gary Payton and Isiah Thomas  to to his mom so I had his mom read a  letter she's kept all of his letters  home from when he was in college at UNC  and this is a homesick Michael Jordan  writing a letter home to his mom to say  he doesn't have 20 dollars to his name  and he needs money for stamps and she  read that out loud and I showed him an  iPad of her reading the letter out loud  and and he got emotional this is a human  being you know we see this guy as a  two-dimensional figure on our wall he's  a logo on a shoe that's not human but  when you show him his mom reading a  letter home and you remind him of what  he came from and that it wasn't always  this way wasn't always sitting in a  mansion telling stories about his life  that are going to be that are going to  be absorbed all over the world he used  to just be Mike Jordan from Wilmington  North Carolina so reminding him of that  I think that that's a comfortable place  for him to go back to yeah and you do  such a great job getting reactions  that's what I love about your  documentaries and and and so you're  rolling constantly you want to get  Jordans reaction no matter what he's  doing I get that reaction you got that  reaction when he talked about I said  Thomas and it still bothers him how many  years later who's going to be upset with  the way they're portrayed in this  if they're upset they're going to be  upset because they were portrayed  honestly I'll say that but I think that  I don't think anyone was treated  unfairly put it that way  III put my name on the line here to tell  an honest with that said who is it I  there we asked for Isaiah today because  I I know that that is so deep-rooted  with Mike and the Pistons and how they  treated him then lamb beer comes out on  the mother ship a couple of days ago and  says LeBrons the greatest player of all  time  this angle they're taking this to their  their graves how they feel about one  another they really are yeah I think  that there's a mutual respect there and  they'll acknowledge one another but it's  deep man this is this is not the Banana  Boat MBA  this is rough-and-tumble eighties  nineties MBA how much pressure do you  feel none I felt pressure at the outset  to get this thing done but but we're  almost at the finish line and at this  point I just feel pride I feel pride in  the job that my team has done under  these circumstances especially to get  this thing out and we all feel pride  that we can give people a little bit of  an escape a little bit of a diversion  and what's some surreal times here  there's business there's nothing that  watch there's nothing to talk about that  connection that sports brings us it's  not there so I think all of us feel an  enormous sense of pride that we can do  something we can't donate blood we can't  go give someone a hug we can't go  deliver someone food you can't touch  each other you can't be around each  other but this is what we can do to  connect with people so we're all really  proud of that Congrats Jason and well  done with the Bears documentary and of  course the Fab five as well we  appreciate your time and we'll be  watching Sunday night on the mothership  at 9:00 Eastern  Thank You Jays thanks a lot guys that's  a Jason here  he's the director of ESPN's the last  dance documentary

